decision_conditions:
Prior to the commencement of development, detailed drawings and specifications for all foundations and associated construction works within the Root Protection Area (RPA) of any retained tree shall be submitted to and approved in writing by the Local Planning Authority.
The submitted details shall include:
1.	A site-specific foundation design prepared in accordance with BS5837:2012 Trees in Relation to Design, Demolition and Construction – Recommendations, demonstrating how adverse impacts on retained trees will be avoided and minimised. Where construction is proposed within the RPA, the design shall utilise sympathetic and minimally invasive construction techniques, such as screw piles, mini-piles, sleeved piles or other special engineering solutions agreed in writing by the Local Planning Authority.
2.	Full construction details including:
o	Dimensioned plans;
o	Dimensioned cross-sectional drawings;
o	Existing and proposed ground levels.
3.	Details demonstrating that all roots measuring 25mm diameter and over, and all clusters or clumps of roots, irrespective of individual root diameter, shall be retained and protected throughout the construction process. In accordance with BS5837:2012, roots occurring in clumps, or roots measuring 25mm diameter and greater, shall only be severed following consultation with a suitably qualified arboriculturist and prior written approval from the Local Planning Authority.
4.	An updated Tree Protection Plan (TPP) and Arboricultural Method Statement (AMS) reflecting the approved foundation design and construction methodology. These documents shall:
o	Illustrate all tree protection measures;
o	Detail all special engineering works within the RPA;
o	Include supervision and monitoring requirements;
o	Demonstrate compliance with Sections 5.5, 6.1, 6.2, 6.3 and 7 of BS5837:2012.
The development shall thereafter be carried out strictly in accordance with the approved details, AMS and TPP unless otherwise agreed in writing by the Local Planning Authority.

Reason: To safeguard the health, longevity, structural stability and amenity value of retained protected trees and to ensure that the development does not result in unacceptable damage to retained tree root systems, in accordance with BS5837:2012 Trees in Relation to Design, Demolition and Construction – Recommendations.
